<p class="auto-style16 beeline-colored-area beeline-theme-bright lt-chem-36406">When drawing the polymer product of a step-growth polymerization, it is important to first identify the reaction which forms the repetitive linkage between monomers.  In this case, the reaction is anucleophilic acyl substitution btween a carboxylic acid and an amine to form an amide.  In total, the bracket structure of a polymer shows two versions of the linkage formation.  The first is clearly shows connecting the monomers to form the dimer making up the repeating unit in the polymer.  The second version involves bonds going out of the bracket and represents how the linkage repeats throughout the polymer.</p>

